  "facts": [
    "Albert Bridge, opened on 23 August 1873, was the first London bridge to feature a cable‑stayed design, with 32 inclined stays supporting a 117‑metre central span, a daring innovation by Rowland Mason Ordish.",
    "Nicknamed “The Trembling Lady,” the bridge famously vibrated under troop marches, prompting permanent warning signs that still advise soldiers to break step when crossing the 27‑foot‑wide deck.",
    "In 1973, the Greater London Council added two concrete piers, converting the central span into a simple beam and creating today’s unique hybrid of cable‑stayed, suspension, and beam styles."
